### What is the Autocomplete Suggestions problem?

Given a set of words and a typed prefix, return all words that start with that prefix. Optionally, limit the result to the top few (by alphabetical order, frequency, or some other criterion). This is the classic autocomplete feature powered by a Trie.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class AutocompleteSystem {

    static class TrieNode {
        TreeMap<Character, TrieNode> children = new TreeMap<>();
        boolean isWord = false;
    }

    private TrieNode root = new TrieNode();

    public void insert(String word) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
            node = node.children.computeIfAbsent(c, k -> new TrieNode());
        }
        node.isWord = true;
    }

    public List<String> autocomplete(String prefix, int limit) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        for (char c : prefix.toCharArray()) {
            if (!node.children.containsKey(c)) return new ArrayList<>();
            node = node.children.get(c);
        }

        List<String> results = new ArrayList<>();
        collectWords(node, new StringBuilder(prefix), results, limit);
        return results;
    }

    private void collectWords(TrieNode node, StringBuilder current,
                              List<String> results, int limit) {
        if (results.size() >= limit) return;
        if (node.isWord) results.add(current.toString());

        for (var entry : node.children.entrySet()) {
            current.append(entry.getKey());
            collectWords(entry.getValue(), current, results, limit);
            current.deleteCharAt(current.length() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

### How does the two-phase approach work?

Phase 1: Walk from the trie root to the prefix node by following each character. If any character has no matching child, no word starts with that prefix and you return an empty list. Phase 2: Run DFS from the prefix node, appending characters as you descend and recording a complete word each time you pass a node marked as a word end.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class TwoPhaseDemo {

    public static void demonstrate() {
        AutocompleteSystem system = new AutocompleteSystem();
        system.insert("RAJESH");
        system.insert("RAJIV");
        system.insert("PRIYA");
        system.insert("PRANAV");

        // Phase 1: prefix "RAJ" → walk r->a->j, arrive at prefix node
        // Phase 2: DFS from prefix node
        //   → E → S → H (word end) → collect "RAJESH"
        //   → I → V (word end) → collect "RAJIV"

        System.out.println(system.autocomplete("RAJ", 10));
    }
}
```

```text
[RAJESH, RAJIV]
```

### Why use a TreeMap for children?

Using a TreeMap (sorted map) for children means the DFS visits children in alphabetical order, producing suggestions in sorted order naturally. If you use a HashMap, suggestions come in arbitrary order and need sorting afterward.

```java
// TreeMap children: DFS visits 'a' before 'b' before 'c'
//   Suggestions come out alphabetically: ["RAJESH", "RAJIV"]

// HashMap children: DFS visits in hash order
//   Suggestions may come as ["RAJIV", "RAJESH"]
//   Need to sort afterward
```

### How does the limit work?

The early return `if (results.size() >= limit) return;` at the top of the DFS method stops exploration once enough suggestions are gathered. This avoids scanning the entire subtree when only the top few are needed.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class LimitDemo {

    public static void demonstrate() {
        AutocompleteSystem system = new AutocompleteSystem();
        system.insert("RAJESH");
        system.insert("RAJIV");
        system.insert("RAJENDRA");
        system.insert("RAJAT");

        // Limit 2: stops after collecting 2 suggestions
        System.out.println(system.autocomplete("RAJ", 2));
        // With TreeMap: ["RAJAT", "RAJENDRA"] (alphabetical, first 2)
    }
}
```

```text
[RAJAT, RAJENDRA]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Phase | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Build trie | O(total characters) | O(total characters) |
| Walk to prefix | O(prefix length) | O(1) |
| Collect suggestions | O(matched subtree) | O(longest word) for stack |
| With limit | O(prefix length + limit * avg word length) | O(1) per suggestion |

### What about frequency-based suggestions?

To suggest the most frequently searched words, store a frequency count at each word-end node. During collection, either sort by frequency afterward or maintain a top-K min-heap during the DFS. For very large tries, each node can cache its top suggestions to answer instantly.

