### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include an empty prefix (return all words up to the limit), a prefix longer than any word (return empty), a prefix that is itself a complete word (include it in the results), and no words in the trie (return empty).

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static void test() {
        AutocompleteSystem system = new AutocompleteSystem();
        system.insert("RAJ");
        system.insert("RAJESH");

        // Empty prefix: returns all words
        System.out.println(system.autocomplete("", 10));      // [RAJ, RAJESH]

        // Prefix is a word: include it
        System.out.println(system.autocomplete("RAJ", 10));    // [RAJ, RAJESH]

        // Prefix too long: no match
        System.out.println(system.autocomplete("RAJESHKUMAR", 10)); // []
    }
}
```

```text
[RAJ, RAJESH]
[RAJ, RAJESH]
[]
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to include the prefix itself as a suggestion when it is a complete word. Another mistake is not handling the empty prefix case, which should return all words up to the limit.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: skip the prefix node's isWord flag
    // If prefix "RAJ" is itself a word, it should be in the results
    // Fix: check node.isWord before descending into children

    // WRONG: crash on empty prefix
    // Empty prefix means the prefix node is the trie root
    // DFS from root collects all words
}
```

### How is this used in banking?

At the National Bank of India, a teller types the first few letters of a registered payee name in the banking system's search box and expects an instant list of matching payees. The trie is warmed from the database at startup. Keystrokes are served from memory, and hot prefixes are cached in Redis for even faster response.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.List;

public class PayeeSearch {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AutocompleteSystem system = new AutocompleteSystem();
        system.insert("RAJESH KUMAR");
        system.insert("RAJIV SINGH");
        system.insert("PRIYA SHARMA");
        system.insert("PANKAJ GUPTA");

        // Teller types "RAJ"
        List<String> suggestions = system.autocomplete("RAJ", 5);
        System.out.println("Suggestions for 'RAJ': " + suggestions);
    }
}
```

```text
Suggestions for 'RAJ': [RAJESH KUMAR, RAJIV SINGH]
```
